  "correctChoiceId": "c",
  "rationale": "CORRECT (C) — \"5.6 mL\". Work in two steps. <strong>Dose required</strong> = 4 mg/kg × 14 kg = <strong>56 mg</strong>. <strong>Volume</strong> = (want ÷ have) × stock volume = (56 ÷ 50) × 5 mL = <strong>5.6 mL</strong>. Sense-check: 56 mg is slightly more than the 50 mg contained in 5 mL, so the answer must be slightly more than 5 mL — which immediately rules out three of the four options.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — 3.6 mL results from dividing rather than multiplying at the weight step. It is less than the stock volume, which the sense-check rejects.\n• B — 5.0 mL is the stock volume itself — the answer you get if you ignore the child's weight entirely.\n• D — 11.2 mL is double the correct dose, from using 8 mg/kg or multiplying by the stock volume twice. This would be a significant overdose.\n\nReference: Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines — Antimalarials",
  "strategy": "Never do drug maths in one step. Write the required dose in milligrams first, then convert to volume. Before choosing, ask: 'is my answer bigger or smaller than the stock volume, and does that make sense?' That single check catches almost every decimal-point error.",
