  "correctChoiceId": "b",
  "rationale": "CORRECT (B) — \"A patient with sickle cell disease whose oxygen concentrator has stopped and who is now restless\". Loss of the oxygen supply in a patient with sickle cell disease is an <strong>airway and oxygenation emergency</strong>. Hypoxia precipitates further sickling and vaso-occlusive crisis, and new restlessness is an early sign of cerebral hypoxia — not anxiety. Restoring oxygen takes priority over pain, scheduled tasks and comfort needs.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — Pain at 7 out of 10 is significant and must be treated promptly, but it does not threaten oxygenation. Severe pain is urgent; hypoxia is life-threatening.\n• C — A task with a 20-minute window is a scheduling matter. Being 'due' does not make it a priority over a deteriorating patient.\n• D — Toileting is a genuine dignity need and can be delegated, but it is never the first action when another patient is hypoxic.\n\nReference: NMC Ghana scope of practice; Ghana STG — Sickle Cell Disease",
  "strategy": "Apply ABC, then ask what changes if you do nothing for ten minutes. Sort options into 'stable and expected' versus 'new and deteriorating'. Restlessness in a patient who has lost oxygen is a deterioration signal, not a behaviour problem — treat new agitation as hypoxia until proven otherwise.",
