  "correctChoiceId": "c",
  "rationale": "CORRECT (C) — \"\"Tell me more about what makes you feel that way.\"\". An open invitation to explore keeps the focus on the client's experience and, critically, opens the door to a direct risk assessment. Therapeutic responses <strong>explore</strong>; they do not reassure, challenge or hand the client to someone else. Statements of worthlessness after an attempt require immediate exploration, not correction.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — False reassurance dismisses the feeling and closes the conversation, teaching the client that this topic is unwelcome.\n• B — 'Why' questions demand justification and put a distressed client on the defensive.\n• D — Referral may follow, but deferring at the moment the client opens up abandons them and loses the disclosure.\n\nReference: NMC Ghana Therapeutic Communication curriculum",
  "strategy": "Eliminate on sight: anything beginning 'don't worry' or 'you should', any 'why...?' question, and any response that passes the client to another professional. What survives is nearly always the option inviting them to say more.",
