  "rationale": "CORRECT (B) — \"A woman due to receive her second unit of blood this morning\". Blood administration requires <strong>registered nurse verification, ongoing assessment and recognition of transfusion reactions</strong>, and it cannot be delegated regardless of how experienced other team members are. The first fifteen minutes carry the highest risk of a haemolytic reaction, and interpreting early signs is an assessment skill inside the RN's scope alone.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — A mobilising, uncomplicated day-four patient awaiting discharge is stable and predictable — appropriate to delegate with clear reporting instructions.\n• C — Assistance with hygiene and feeding sits squarely within a health care assistant's role and is a reasonable delegation.\n• D — Stable observations on established oral medication describe a predictable patient. Delegating this frees the RN for the transfusion.\n\nReference: NMC Ghana Code of Conduct and Ethics; scope of practice",
  "strategy": "Delegate by predictability, never by seniority. Ask whether the outcome of the next hour is known. If the patient needs first assessment, blood, IV push medication, teaching, or is unstable, the registered nurse keeps them — twelve years of assistant experience does not widen a legal scope of practice.",
