  "rationale": "CORRECT (A) — \"Limit stock cubes, dried salted fish and processed seasoning powders\". Most dietary sodium comes from <strong>added seasonings rather than the salt shaker</strong>. Stock cubes, salted dried fish, shito and seasoning powders contribute heavily in Ghanaian cooking. Reducing these while flavouring with onion, garlic, ginger, pepper and local herbs achieves real reduction without making food unpalatable.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• B — Sugar does not substitute for salt as a flavour and adds energy, which is unhelpful in heart failure.\n• C — Fresh vegetables are very low in sodium and should be encouraged. Avoiding them removes potassium and fibre.\n• D — Extra fluid worsens congestion in heart failure. These patients are often on a fluid restriction instead.\n\nReference: Ghana STG — heart failure; hypertension dietary advice",
