  "correctChoiceId": "d",
  "rationale": "CORRECT (D) — \"Energy-giving foods, valued mainly for their carbohydrate content\". These are all <strong>starchy staples</strong> from maize, cassava and yam, and their main contribution is <strong>carbohydrate for energy</strong>. They form the bulk of most Ghanaian meals. Because they hold little protein and few micronutrients, they must be eaten with a protein source and a vegetable to make the meal adequate.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — These staples contain only small amounts of protein of limited quality, so they cannot meet body-building needs.\n• B — Protective foods are the fruits and vegetables supplying vitamins and minerals, which staples supply very little of.\n• C — Fibre content varies but is not why these foods are eaten. Their role in the diet is to provide energy.\n\nReference: Ghana Health Service — food-based dietary guidelines",
  "strategy": "Classify by the nutrient present in the largest amount. Starchy staples are energy foods, animal and legume foods are body-building, fruits and vegetables are protective.",
