  "rationale": "CORRECT (D) — \"Weigh yourself each morning and report a gain of two kilograms\". A rapid weight gain reflects <strong>retained fluid, not gained tissue</strong>, and rises before breathlessness and swelling appear. Daily morning weighing on the same scale gives the earliest warning that decompensation is starting, allowing the diuretic to be adjusted before admission becomes necessary. It pairs with a low-salt diet.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — Protein is needed to prevent cardiac cachexia, a genuine risk in advanced heart failure. There is no reason to avoid it.\n• B — Extra salt draws water back into the circulation and directly opposes the diuretic, precipitating readmission.\n• C — Large fluid volumes overload a failing heart. Intake is usually moderated rather than encouraged.\n\nReference: Ghana STG — heart failure; patient self-management",
