  "rationale": "CORRECT (B) — \"Fifteen grams of fast-acting glucose, then recheck after fifteen minutes\". For hypoglycaemia in a <strong>conscious patient who can swallow safely</strong>, the treatment is <strong>15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate</strong> — three teaspoons of sugar, glucose tablets or half a bottle of soft drink — then recheck in 15 minutes and repeat if still low. Once above 4 mmol/L, a starchy snack prevents recurrence.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — A mixed meal digests too slowly. It is the correct follow-up once glucose has recovered, not the initial treatment.\n• C — Intravenous dextrose is reserved for the patient who is unconscious or unable to swallow. It is invasive and unnecessary here.\n• D — Hypoglycaemia is an emergency treated on the spot. Waiting for review risks seizure, coma and brain injury.\n\nReference: Ghana STG — diabetes mellitus; hypoglycaemia",
