  "rationale": "CORRECT (C) — \"A falling serum phosphate driven by the insulin surge on refeeding\". Feeding a starved body releases <strong>insulin</strong>, which drives <strong>phosphate, potassium and magnesium into the cells</strong> just as depleted stores are being consumed to make ATP. The resulting hypophosphataemia causes confusion, weakness, arrhythmia and can be fatal. Prevention is to start at a low rate, give thiamine before feeding, and monitor electrolytes daily.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — Sodium usually falls rather than rises in refeeding, as fluid is retained. Hypernatraemia does not explain this picture.\n• B — Calcium is not released from bone by refeeding, and hypercalcaemia is not a feature of the syndrome.\n• D — A falling urea reflects anabolism and is a favourable sign. It does not cause confusion or arrhythmia.\n\nReference: Ghana STG — severe malnutrition; refeeding precautions",
