  "rationale": "CORRECT (C) — \"A patient with a high-output small bowel fistula and short remaining gut\". The governing rule is <strong>if the gut works, use it</strong>. Enteral feeding preserves the mucosal barrier, costs less and carries fewer infective risks. Parenteral nutrition is reserved for a gut that <strong>cannot be used or cannot absorb</strong> — a high-output fistula with insufficient remaining bowel is exactly that situation.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — An unsafe swallow with a working bowel is the classic indication for nasogastric or gastrostomy feeding, not intravenous nutrition.\n• B — Food refusal in dementia calls for feeding assistance and a careful ethical discussion, not parenteral nutrition.\n• D — A patient with wired jaws can take liquid feeds orally or by fine-bore tube. The bowel is intact and should be used.\n\nReference: Ghana STG — nutritional support; route selection",
