  "rationale": "CORRECT (D) — \"Iron, because of menstrual losses combined with rapid growth\". Adolescent girls face a <strong>double demand</strong>: the growth spurt expands blood volume and muscle mass while <strong>menstruation causes regular iron loss</strong>. Diets built on starchy staples supply mainly non-haem iron, which is poorly absorbed. This is why weekly iron and folic acid supplementation is offered through schools.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• A — Vitamin K deficiency is a newborn concern. Gut bacteria and green vegetables supply enough in older children and adults.\n• B — B12 comes from animal foods rather than cereals, and clinical deficiency is uncommon in this group in Ghana.\n• C — Phosphorus is abundant in ordinary diets and dietary deficiency is rare outside refeeding and severe illness.\n\nReference: Ghana Health Service — adolescent health and nutrition",
