  "rationale": "CORRECT (A) — \"Using separate boards and knives for raw meat and ready-to-eat food\". Cross-contamination is the transfer of organisms from <strong>raw food to food that will not be cooked again</strong>. Separating equipment breaks that route, as does storing raw meat below ready-to-eat items, washing hands between tasks and using clean cloths. Cooking kills bacteria, so anything eaten without further cooking must never share surfaces with raw meat.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• B — Rinsing raw chicken splashes Campylobacter around the sink and surfaces. It is actively discouraged; cooking is what kills the organism.\n• C — Raw meat on a top shelf drips onto everything beneath. It belongs on the bottom shelf in a covered container.\n• D — A cloth used all day becomes the vehicle that spreads contamination from surface to surface.\n\nReference: Ghana Food and Drugs Authority — catering hygiene",
