  "correctChoiceId": "a",
  "rationale": "CORRECT (A) — \"They lower the water available so that microorganisms cannot grow\". Both methods work by reducing <strong>water activity</strong>. Microorganisms need free water to multiply; salt draws it out osmotically and drying removes it by evaporation, so growth halts. The organisms are <strong>inhibited rather than killed</strong>, which is why rehydrated fish spoils quickly and why moulds can still grow if drying is incomplete.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• B — Salting and drying do not raise pH. Preservation by acidity is a different method, used in fermentation and pickling.\n• C — No protective protein layer is formed. Surface changes in drying do not constitute a microbial barrier.\n• D — No antibiotic is added or produced. Nothing is killed; the conditions for growth are simply removed.\n\nReference: Ghana nursing curriculum — food science and preservation",
