  "rationale": "CORRECT (A) — \"Aflatoxin is heat stable and survives roasting, and it damages the liver\". Aspergillus species growing on poorly dried groundnuts and maize produce <strong>aflatoxin</strong>, a <strong>heat-stable toxin that ordinary cooking does not destroy</strong>. Chronic exposure causes liver damage and hepatocellular carcinoma, acts together with hepatitis B, and impairs child growth. Prevention is proper drying, dry storage and discarding discoloured or shrivelled nuts.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• B — The physical state of the mould is irrelevant. The danger is a chemical toxin already present in the nut.\n• C — Loss of nutritional value is trivial compared with the carcinogenic and hepatotoxic risk of the toxin.\n• D — Fungi do not become bacteria. The harm comes from a toxin, not from any living organism in the gut.\n\nReference: Ghana Food and Drugs Authority — mycotoxin control",
