  "rationale": "CORRECT (A) — \"Replace sugary drinks and fried snacks in the canteen and add activity\". Rising childhood obesity is a <strong>population problem</strong> best met with environmental change: altering what the canteen sells, providing safe drinking water, and building physical activity into the school day. Such measures reach every child, avoid stigma, and change the setting rather than relying on individual willpower.\n\nWhy the other options fail:\n• B — Public weighing humiliates children, encourages disordered eating and changes nothing about the food available.\n• C — Skipping breakfast impairs concentration and learning and is associated with higher, not lower, obesity risk.\n• D — Individual referral overwhelms the service and treats a whole-population issue one child at a time.\n\nReference: Ghana Health Service — school health programme; non-communicable disease prevention",
  "strategy": "When a problem affects a whole group, the strongest answer changes the environment rather than instructing individuals. Reserve individual referral for individual pathology.",
